\label{propc9}
The local PSS and local SSP maps are chain homotopy inverses to each other. As a consequence, one has a canonical isomorphism
\begin{equation*}
H({\mathcal F}; {\mathfrak o}) \cong HF^{\rm loc}(\phi, \overline{\mathcal F}; {\mathbb Z}).
\end{equation*}
When $\phi$ has finite order, the local system ${\mathfrak o}$ is trivial.

\label{propc11}
Let $\phi$ be a finite-order Hamiltonian diffeomorphism on $(M, \omega)$ generated by a Hamiltonian $H$ and $p$ be a prime which does not divide the order of $\phi$. Let ${\mathcal F}$ be a connected component of ${\rm Fix}_c(\phi)$. Then up to a global degree shift there is an isomorphism of ${\mathcal{R}_p}$-modules
\begin{equation*}
HF_{{\mathbb{Z}/p}}^{{\rm loc}}(H^{(p)}, \overline{\mathcal F}{}^{(p)}) \cong H({\mathcal F}; {{\mathbb F}_p}) \otimes {\mathcal{R}_p}.
\end{equation*}

The proof is similar to that of Proposition \ref{propc9}. Let $(f_{\mathcal F}, h_{\mathcal F})$ be a Morse--Smale pair on ${\mathcal F}$ which induces a Morse complex $CM({\mathcal F}; {{\mathbb F}_p})$. Let $G$ be a $C^\infty$-close nondegenerate perturbation of $H$ such that $G^{(p)}$ is also nondegenerate. Let $\sigma$ be a Hamiltonian connection on ${\mathbb R}\times S^1$ interpolating from $H$ to $G$, which induces a Hamiltonian connection $\sigma^{(p)}$ interpolating from $H^{(p)}$ to  $G^{(p)}$.  Then for each $y \in {\rm crit}(f_{\mathcal F})\subset {\mathcal F}$ and $\overline{x} \in \tilde {\mathcal O}(G^{(p)})$, one can consider a similar moduli space as the one used in the proof of Proposition \ref{propc9}, with $\sigma$ replaced by $\sigma^{(p)}$.

Moreover, to count solutions equivariantly, couple the equation by parametrized gradient flow lines of $g$ in $S^\infty$. Then for $w_-, w_+ \in {\rm crit}(g)$ and $y \in {\rm crit}(f_{\mathcal F})$, $\overline{x}\in \tilde {\mathcal O}(G^{(p)}, \overline{\mathcal F}{}^{(p)})$, one has a moduli space
\begin{equation*}
{\mathcal M}_{w_-, w_+}(y, \overline{x})
\end{equation*}
of pairs. As $\sigma^{(p)}$ has small curvature, the usual energy identity together with Lemma \ref{lemmac5} implies that the moduli space can be compactified by only adding local broken configurations without sphere bubbles. Still, one does not have automatic ${\mathbb Z}_{\geq 0} \times {\mathbb{Z}/p}$-equivariant transversality. One could, however, achieve transversality by virtual perturbations which respect the symmetry. As there are no sphere bubbles, one can use single-valued perturbations and obtain integral counts. Then one obtains a ${\mathbb{Z}/p}$-equivariant cochain map
\begin{equation*}
CM({\mathcal F};{\mathbb F}_p) \otimes CM(g) \to CF^{{\rm loc}}(G^{(p)}, \overline{\mathcal F}{}^{(p)}) \otimes CM(g).
\end{equation*}
Its restriction to the ${\mathbb{Z}/p}$-invariant part is 
\begin{equation*}
CM({\mathcal F}; {\mathcal{R}_p}) \to CF_{\mathbb{Z}/p}^{\rm loc} (G^{(p)}, \overline{\mathcal F}{}^{(p)})
\end{equation*}
and hence induces the PSS map
\begin{equation*}
{\it PSS}_{p, {\mathcal F}}^{\rm loc}: H({\mathcal F}; {\mathcal{R}_p}) \to HF_{\mathbb{Z}/p}^{\rm loc}( H^{(p)}, \overline{\mathcal F}{}^{(p)}).
\end{equation*}
The standard method proves that this map is ${\mathcal{R}_p}$-linear.

One can also define a similar SSP map
\begin{equation*}
{\it SSP}_{p, {\mathcal F}}^{\rm loc}: HF_{\mathbb{Z}/p}^{\rm loc}( H^{(p)}, \overline{\mathcal F}{}^{(p)} ) \to H({\mathcal F}; {\mathcal{R}_p}).
\end{equation*}
To prove that the PSS and SSP maps have composition in either direction being invertible, one considers the two different gluings. As it is true for the non-equivariant versions, the equivariant version only differs by terms with positive $(t, \theta)$-degrees. Therefore, the claimed isomorphism can be induced by ${\it PSS}_{p, {\mathcal F}}^{\rm loc}$.

\label{propc13}
The following diagram commutes. 
\begin{equation*}
\xymatrix{ HF^{{\rm loc}}(H, \overline{\mathcal F}) \ar[rr]^-{{\it FSt}_{p, \overline{\mathcal F}}^{\rm loc}}   &  &  HF^{{\rm loc}}_{{\mathbb{Z}/p}}(H^{(p)}, \overline{\mathcal F}{}^{(p)}) \ar[d]^{{\it SSP}_{{\mathbb{Z}/p}, \overline{\mathcal F}}^{\rm loc}}  \\
