\end{equation*}

The proof is based on the crossing energy estimate and gluing (at nondegenerate Hamiltonian orbits). Let $G$ be a nondegenerate perturbation of $H$. On the cochain level, one needs to verify the commutativity of 
\begin{equation}\label{eqn51}
\vcenter{ \xymatrix{
CF(G, \overline{\mathcal F})^{\otimes p} \otimes CM(g)  \ar[rr]^-{\widetilde{\widetilde{{\it FSt}}}{}_{p, \overline{\mathcal F}}^{\rm loc}} & &   CF(G^{(p)}, \overline{\mathcal F}{}^{(p)}) \otimes CM(g) \ar[d]^{\widetilde{\widetilde{{\it SSP}}}{}_{{\mathbb{Z}/p}, {\mathcal F}}^{\rm loc}}\\
CM({\mathcal F})^{\otimes p} \otimes CM(g)  \ar[rr] \ar[u]^{({\it PSS}_{{\mathcal F}}^{\rm loc})^{\otimes p} \otimes {\rm Id}}  & &  CM({\mathcal F}) \otimes CM(g).
}}
\end{equation}
Here the unlabelled arrow, which is supposed to induce the classical Steenrod operation on ${\mathcal F}$, has not been explicitly defined yet.

One uses a typical TQFT+gluing argument to prove the commutativity. The triple composition
\begin{equation*}
\widetilde{\widetilde{{\it SSP}}}{}_{{\mathbb{Z}/p}, {\mathcal F}}^{\rm loc} \circ \widetilde{\widetilde{{\it FSt}}}^{{\rm loc}}_{p, \overline{\mathcal F}} \circ ( ( {\it PSS}_{\mathcal F}^{\rm loc})^{\otimes p} \otimes {\rm Id})
\end{equation*}
can be identified, via gluings at $p$ negative ends resp. one positive end and at (nondegenerate) orbits of $G$ resp. $G^{(p)}$ as well as critical points of $g$, to the map defined via the count of configurations where one has $p$ incoming gradient rays and one outgoing gradient ray on ${\mathcal F}$, between which is a solution to the Floer equation on $\Sigma$ being ${\mathbb C}$ removing $p$-th roots of unity with flat Hamiltonian connection equal to the pullback of $H dt$ from ${\mathbb R}\times S^1$. The crossing energy estimate can guarantee the gluing happens ``locally'' and the Floer equation on $\Sigma$ must be a constant one. Therefore, the configurations one finally obtains are those Morse gradient trees responsible for the classical Steenrod operation on ${\mathcal F}$. The original commutative diagram is then obtained by 1) restricting \eqref{eqn51} to the ${\mathbb{Z}/p}$-invariant part, 2) reducing to cohomology, and 3) composing with the quasi-Frobenius maps.

